    [
     1,
     "Hey, Noam.  That IS a nice example, but it&#39;s worth mentioning that the\r<br>&quot;pipe&quot; operator (which Hadley pronounces something like &quot;the-in&quot;\r<br>[&quot;then&quot;]) is actually part of the &quot;magrittr&quot; package.  I.e., dplyr\r<br>makes effective use of the concept, but you don&#39;t have to use dplyr to\r<br>benefit from it.\r<br>\r<br>-- Mike\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Tue, Sep 30, 2014 at 2:39 PM, Noam Ross &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">noam...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hello all,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; The votes are in, and fall D-RUG sessions will be held Tuesdays 1:10-3 in\r<br>&gt; Wickson 2124. Our first meeting will be next week, on Tuesday, Oct 7.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; For those new to D-RUG, our work sessions are times for you to come to work\r<br>&gt; on your own projects (including classwork) and get help on R and related\r<br>&gt; topics from the D-RUG community. We have speakers every other week. These\r<br>&gt; talks take up the first hour or less, leaving the rest of the time open for\r<br>&gt; co-work and help.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Next week, Michael Levy will give a talk on how to use the dplyr package to\r<br>&gt; make your R code easier to write and read (and faster, too!). With dplyr,\r<br>&gt; you can turn this:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; crime.by.state &lt;- read.csv(&quot;CrimeStatebyState.csv&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; crime.ny.2005 &lt;- crime.by.state[crime.by.state$Year==2005 &amp;\r<br>&gt;                                   crime.by.state$State==&quot;New York&quot;,\r<br>&gt;                                 c(&quot;Type.of.Crime&quot;, &quot;Count&quot;)]\r<br>&gt; crime.ny.2005 &lt;- crime.ny.2005[order(crime.ny.2005$Count,\r<br>&gt;                                      decreasing=TRUE), ]\r<br>&gt; crime.ny.2005$Proportion &lt;- crime.ny.2005$Count /\r<br>&gt;                             sum(crime.ny.2005$Count)\r<br>&gt; summary1 &lt;- aggregate(Count ~ Type.of.Crime,\r<br>&gt;                       data=crime.ny.2005,\r<br>&gt;                       FUN=sum)\r<br>&gt; summary2 &lt;- aggregate(Count ~ Type.of.Crime,\r<br>&gt;                       data=crime.ny.2005,\r<br>&gt;                       FUN=length)\r<br>&gt; final &lt;- merge(summary1, summary2,\r<br>&gt;                by=&quot;Type.of.Crime&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; into this:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; library(dplyr)\r<br>&gt; crime.by.state &lt;- read.csv(&quot;CrimeStatebyState.csv&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; final &lt;- crime.by.state %&gt;%\r<br>&gt;            filter(State==&quot;New York&quot;, Year==2005) %&gt;%\r<br>&gt;            arrange(desc(Count)) %&gt;%\r<br>&gt;            select(Type.of.Crime, Count) %&gt;%\r<br>&gt;            mutate(Proportion=Count/sum(Count)) %&gt;%\r<br>&gt;            group_by(Type.of.Crime) %&gt;%\r<br>&gt;            summarise(num.types = n(), counts = sum(Count))\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Much better, no? Michael will show you more! See you on Tuesday.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Noam\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; *Thanks to Tony Fischetti for the example\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
